"""        <h2>Useful Phrases <span class="em">🧰</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Ten phrases for delivering a slipped deadline — to clients, bosses, colleagues, in-laws expecting you at six. Say each out loud BEFORE you reveal it.</p>

        <div class="deliver"><b>Say it like you mean it.</b> The whole call rests on one exchange rate: bad news early is cheap, bad news late is ruinous. Deliver the headline in your first breath — every sentence of preamble reads as squirming — and never, ever give a second date you aren't certain of. The first slip costs a deadline. The second costs the relationship.</div>

        <h3>📢 The headline, first</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 1</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> opening the call. Which version?</p>
            <div class="choice">
                <div><b>A.</b> "So, it's been a really challenging couple of weeks, and I want to give you the full context before—"</div>
                <div><b>B.</b> "That's why I'm calling — I'd rather tell you now than on Friday."</div>
                <div><b>C.</b> "Everything's mostly on track! Just one or two small timing things to flag."</div>
            </div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the answer</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>B — "I'd rather tell you now than on Friday."</b><br>A is the excuse-story arriving before the news — and the listener knows something's wrong from word one, so the preamble reads as squirming. C is worse: minimising language ("small timing things") that will be quoted back to you bitterly next week. B does two jobs in nine words: warns that bad news is coming, and frames the call itself as an act of respect — you're paying for the news with earliness. Which is exactly the currency it costs.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'd rather tell you now than ___." The opener that makes early delivery of bad news part of the message.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 2</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the news itself, unpadded. Complete it:<br><br>"The honest ___ is: we're not going to make the fifteenth."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"The honest answer is: we're not going to make the fifteenth."</b><br>Say the actual sentence. Not "we may be looking at some slippage", not "the timeline's under pressure" — the plain fact, with the date in it. "The honest answer is" earns its place: it signals you've stopped negotiating with reality and are reporting it, which is what lets the listener stop wondering what you're softening. Then attach the new date in the same breath — bad news without a new date is just panic, shared.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "The honest answer is: ___." For every status update tempted to blur itself.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 3</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the reason, in one line, refused as an excuse. Complete it:<br><br>"I'll spare you the long version — the one thing that ___ is ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I'll spare you the long version — the one thing that matters is the supplier delivered wrong parts."</b><br>The client deserves the cause; they don't deserve the saga — sagas are for sympathy, and you're not calling for sympathy. One line of reason, then the crucial move: re-owning the promise anyway. "The supplier is my problem, not your excuse to absorb." Blame passed down the chain impresses nobody; a supplier who owns the whole promise, including the parts others broke, is the one who gets the next contract.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'll spare you the long version — the one thing that matters is ___." Then own the promise, whoever broke it.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🔧 Shrinking the damage</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 4</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the question that resizes the whole problem. Complete it:<br><br>"What does this actually ___ at your end?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"What does this actually break at your end?"</b><br>The turning point of the whole call — and the question guilty suppliers never ask, because they're too busy apologising to find out what they're apologising for. Your deadline is a means to THEIR end, and their end may need less than you think: here, one working unit for a Monday board, not the full delivery. You can't offer the rescue until you know the real damage. Ask, then be quiet and listen.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "What does this actually break at your end?" Every delay, every cancellation, every change of plan — measure the real damage before you price the apology.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 5</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the partial rescue. Complete it:<br><br>"Which part do you need ___? Because that part you can have on ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Which part do you need first? Because that part you can have on time."</b><br>Deadlines feel like one thing; deliveries almost never are. Somewhere in your late shipment is an on-time piece — the working unit, the draft chapter, the headline figures — and offering it converts "everything is late" into "the thing you need is on time; the rest follows". This is not spin: it's logistics done with the client's actual Monday in mind. It saved this contract in one sentence.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Which part do you need first?" Split every late delivery into the urgent piece and the rest — then land the piece.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 6</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> making the new date believable — the second date is sacred. Put the pieces in order:</p>
            <div class="jumble"><span>I can stand behind.</span><span>Thursday is a date</span><span>Not hope for —</span><span>stand behind.</span></div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Thursday is a date I can stand behind. Not hope for — stand behind."</b><br>Her scepticism was earned — "the last supplier who gave me a revised date revised it twice more." A first slip is forgivable; a slipped RESCUE date is fatal, because it proves the problem isn't the schedule, it's your relationship with truth. So the new date must be built differently: on things that exist ("parts physically in my workshop"), with margin in it, and named as a commitment, not a forecast. If you're not certain, give the date you ARE certain of — even if it's uglier.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "That's a date I can stand behind." Only ever say it about one you can.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>💷 Paying for it before you're asked</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 7</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> defending the delay itself. Complete it:<br><br>"I'd rather lose a week than hand you something ___-___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I'd rather lose a week than hand you something half-done."</b><br>The one moment the delay gets defended — not excused, defended — because the alternative to late is usually worse: on-time and broken, followed by a month of apologising in instalments. Clients who've lived that (most have — "that, I've lived") hear this sentence as maturity, not spin. It only works if it's true, and it's only true if the extra week actually produces the finished thing. Which is also the test of whether to say it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'd rather be late than hand over something half-done." The quality defence — use sparingly, mean entirely.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 8</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> killing the chasing before it starts. Complete it:<br><br>"You'll get updates ___ asking — every morning until it ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"You'll get updates without asking — every morning until it lands."</b><br>After a slip, silence is poison — every quiet day, the client wonders if it's happening again, and every chase they have to send costs you a point of trust. The fix is a broadcast schedule: fixed rhythm, two lines, even when the news is boring — ESPECIALLY when the news is boring, because "no change, still Thursday" is exactly what rebuilds the credit. The updates aren't information. They're evidence you've changed.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "You'll get updates without asking — every ___ until it's done." The standing repair for every trust you've dented.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 9</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> pricing your own failure, unprompted. Complete it:<br><br>"This one's on us — you'll see that on the ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"This one's on us — you'll see that on the invoice."</b><br>The move almost nobody makes: compensation offered before it's demanded. The amount matters less than the direction — money moving towards the client, uninvited, says the apology has weight behind it. And there's cold logic under the grace: "I'd rather price my own mistake than have you price it for me." Self-imposed penalties are almost always cheaper than extracted ones — and infinitely cheaper than the silent penalty of a client who just stops calling.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "This one's on us — you'll see it on the invoice." When it's your miss, reach for your wallet before they reach for theirs.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 10</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the standing promise that closes the call. Say it out loud, then write it.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="How do you guarantee she'll never learn bad news late from you again?"></textarea>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal a strong version</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>e.g. "If anything moves by even a day, you'll hear it from me the same morning."</b><br>The call ends by turning this one painful Tuesday into policy: not "sorry about this time" but "here is how information will reach you from now on, permanently." It's the promise that makes the whole episode net-positive — her strange admission ("suppliers who delivered on time left me trusting them less than this call") is what it buys. Then comes the only hard part: keeping it, forever, including the mornings you'd rather not dial.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "If anything moves, you'll hear it from me the same day." The standing promise — the whole lesson in one sentence.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="keep">
            <h3>🔑 Make them yours</h3>
            <p>These ten turn your worst professional Tuesdays into trust. Say each answer out loud before you type it.</p>
            <p class="cue">"I'd rather tell you now than Friday." — what news are you currently sitting on, at work or at home, that's getting more expensive by the day?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The news, and who's owed the early call…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"What does this actually break at your end?" — for that same situation: what do you actually know about the other side's real damage?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="What you know, what you've assumed…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"A date I can stand behind." — what's your honest, margin-included date for the thing you're late on? Not the hoped one.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The real date, built on what exists…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="english_at_work_phrase_bank.html">🧰 <span>Every phrase from every English at Work lesson, sorted by what it does.</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <div class="tip"><b>Quick test:</b> the call, out loud — rather tell you now than Friday → the honest answer is → spare you the long version, the promise was mine → what does this break at your end → which part do you need first → a date I can stand behind → rather lose a week than half-done → updates without asking → on us, on the invoice → anything moves, same morning. Ten sentences; one client who trusts you MORE than before you slipped.</div>""",

